[{:seon.task/name "a-stalled-eval-ends-with-its-error", :seon.task/plan "Found by context, 2026-11-13 (evidence/context-refine-hang-2026-11-13.md): a refine's turn-9 eval ran grep -rl over its checkout and blocked in open(2) for 80 minutes; the agent proc recorded it :stalled {:quiet-ms .. :last-op {:kind :tests}}, and nothing ended the eval or answered it, so the run waited until a person ended the grep. Seen again 2026-11-14 (context, seon.turn's task): agent o8c24b62c51eec13 sat :busy 35 minutes at 0% CPU and no stall was recorded at all. Nothing may hang (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs). Owner: runtime (the Flow specialist; plan item 3, the deadline slice). What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: root's api proc arms a polling timer every stall-ms/2 while any agent is busy (seon.system ::stall-timer, system.clj:1034 and :1064-1067; :stall-check at :939-941 sends {:op :stall?} to each busy agent proc); the agent proc answers it (agent_proc.clj:122-126) only when no effect is outstanding, (zero? (:fx-out st 0)), so a wait inside an effect, a model call or a test run is never a stall (the hidden case above); a stall writes {:op :stalled} -> :seon.agent/stalled fact, stall-task and its evidence (system.clj:276-304, :929-937) and ends nothing. Evidence is captured twice: seon.fx/stall-evidence's inline zsh (fx.clj:1832) and bin/evidence. A non-streaming effect already has its declared bound: seon.agent/fx! passes :seon.config.fx/bounds' :seon.bound/ms to await-reply, which arms seon.step/put-after! and answers :seon.bound/reached (agent.clj:235-260, :493-510); a streaming :model call is bounded by its owner's quiet bound (seon.fx/stream-due). The cause: the stall is decided by a clock in root polling quiet frames, not by each request's own declared bound, and an eval's own child process (a grep, a test run) has no bound at all. The design (today's rulings: every wait is a :seon.bound/bound via seon.step/put-after!, :seon.config.fx/bounds; no timers outside Flow; the stall-check poll and :stall? exemption deleted): (1) every request the agent proc takes carries its effective :seon.bound/bound (the least per key of its schema's malli :default, the request's own, and a config override through the one resolver; an eval's default :seon.bound/ms is :seon.config.agent/stall-ms, kept as that default, never a second key); the agent proc arms one put-after! due per request on its own in-port when it takes it, and cancels it on the answer; (2) past it, evidence first: bin/evidence of the agent's process tree (ps, lsof, sample, SIGINFO dumps) through the :evidence effect (stall-evidence's inline script deleted, it calls bin/evidence), the path riding the error; (3) then the end: the eval's process group killed (seon.lib/kill!), the eval answered :seon.agent/stalled-wait {:seon.bound/bound :waited-ms :kind :evidence :children}, never retried; the turn goes on and context's turn records it as the stall (seon.turn's task reads it). The same {:op :stalled}, :seon.agent/stalled fact and stall-task stay as the record. Deletes: the api's ::stall-timer, :stall-check and the stall-ms it carries (system.clj:939-941, :1034-1035, :1064-1067), the agent proc's :stall? clause and its :fx-out exemption (agent_proc.clj:119-129), ::stall-armed and ::busy in stall-state (system.clj:256-273, the read keeps :inflight with each request's due), stall-evidence's inline script. Replaces seon.system/a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound (deleted for it, 2026-11-14) and completes port (1) of seon.step/every-wait-past-a-bound-is-a-flow-message-from-the-one-timer. Estimate: one put-after! per request (~1k ticks) and one cancel, against today's poll of every busy agent every stall-ms/2; no proc woken while nothing is due; per stall one capture (~1-2 s wall, once); the pure step test ~1M ticks, one in-agent run ~3M. Proofs, red first: seon.agent-proc-test/a-request-past-its-bound-is-stalled-with-no-poll (the agent proc alone: a request with :seon.bound/ms 5 gets its stall from its own due, no :stall? message sent; red today: nothing fires without root's poll); seon.agent-test/a-stalled-eval-is-answered-with-its-error (an eval whose child reads a FIFO nobody writes, a short stated bound: answered :seon.agent/stalled-wait with an evidence path holding its lsof, the child gone, the next eval runs; a busy eval under its bound untouched); seon.agent-test/a-model-call-that-never-answers-is-a-stall (the scripted provider never answers: the stall is recorded, red today by the exemption); design guard seon.main-test/no-proc-polls-a-clock (a law over src: no step re-arms a timer to look at elapsed time, (- now last-...) against a bound; every deadline is a put-after! armed with its request).", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-proc-test/a-request-past-its-bound-is-stalled-with-no-poll seon.agent-test/a-model-call-that-never-answers-is-a-stall seon.agent-test/a-stalled-eval-is-answered-with-its-error seon.main-test/no-proc-polls-a-clock}}]
